TESTDATA["pet5-t2"] = {"title":"Cambridge PET 5 · Test 2","format":"2010","reading":[{"part":1,"items":[{"n":1,"text":"Jennie,\nThe garage rang – your new tyres have arrived. They can't fit them until next week. Please let them know today which day will be convenient.","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","q":"Jennie has to","options":["ask another garage to fit her tyres.","arrange a time for the garage to fit the new tyres.","collect the new tyres from the garage."],"answer":"B"},{"n":2,"text":"Would anyone who knows anything about the damaged window in the school library please report to my office before the end of the day. Mrs Swan","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","q":"What does Mrs Swan want to do today?","options":["repair damage done to the library","discover how a window got broken","find out who uses the library"],"answer":"B"},{"n":3,"text":"FOR SALE\nGravulas Computer Game\n(ages 8 and above)\nUnwanted gift – box unopened\n£28 2137","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","q":"","options":["The owner of the computer game that is for sale has never used it.","The computer game is for sale because the owner is too old for it.","The person selling the computer game no longer wants to play with it."],"answer":"A"},{"n":4,"text":"Students have free internet access until midday on weekdays and all day at weekends","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","q":"","options":["There is a charge for internet access at weekends.","Students must always pay to use the internet in the afternoons.","It doesn't cost anything to use the internet on weekday mornings."],"answer":"C"},{"n":5,"text":"Guess who I met on this mountain! My tennis hero! I was breathless because of the climb, so unfortunately couldn't speak to him for a photo of us together. Amanda","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","q":"Amanda regrets that she","options":["didn't recognise her tennis hero from his photo.","couldn't climb high enough to photograph her tennis hero.","didn't have her photograph taken with her tennis hero."],"answer":"C"}]},{"part":2,"people":[{"n":6,"name":"Stephen","text":"Stephen is looking for a top-quality hotel which is convenient for the airport, to hold a meeting with visiting German publishers. They will stay overnight and want to take some exercise outdoors after the meeting.","answer":"D"},{"n":7,"name":"Karl and Monika","text":"Karl and Monika want to stay in the city centre overnight at a hotel offering good local food. The next morning they plan to see the main sights. They are not worried about the cost of the hotel.","answer":"B"},{"n":8,"name":"James and Denise","text":"James and Denise want a modern, medium-priced hotel in the city, but will eat out during their stay. They also want to see some films in the evenings, somewhere near their hotel.","answer":"E"},{"n":9,"name":"David and Katrina","text":"David and Katrina have just started work after leaving college and haven't got much money, so they want a reasonably priced hotel. They like country walks and watersports.","answer":"G"},{"n":10,"name":"Sue and Belinda","text":"Sue and Belinda want to stay somewhere in the city centre that offers a variety of evening entertainment within the hotel, including live music.","answer":"F"}],"texts":[{"letter":"A","title":"The Salisbury Hotel","text":"The Salisbury Hotel is a top hotel with a health club, swimming pool, shops and a fully-equipped business centre. Within the hotel are three international restaurants, one with a French chef. The hotel is conveniently located close to the motorway, though airport users should allow plenty of time because traffic is usually heavy."},{"letter":"B","title":"The Cumberland Hotel","text":"The Cumberland Hotel is well placed for sightseeing on a busy city street, in a district which is full of interesting shops. Rooms are expensive but comfortable and the hotel serves excellent food, typical of the area. A piano player entertains guests every night in the bar."},{"letter":"C","title":"The Rathmore Hotel","text":"The Rathmore Hotel offers good value accommodation, with wonderful English food in the restaurant. The hotel is well-known for its small orchestra which plays while guests have dinner. It is on the eastern edge of the city but special sightseeing buses are available to take guests into the centre."},{"letter":"D","title":"The Russell Hotel","text":"The Russell Hotel is close to the airport, and has quiet, comfortable rooms. However, the journey to the city centre can take time and prices are above average. Delicious local food is served in the restaurant, and its conference rooms and business facilities are excellent. The hotel is surrounded by woodland, offers a golf course, and there are pleasant walks around the nearby lake."},{"letter":"E","title":"The newly-built Aviemore Hotel","text":"The newly-built Aviemore Hotel is small but in the centre of the city's cinema, restaurant and nightclub district. Rooms are clean, comfortable and reasonably priced, although the food is rather basic. There is an electronic games arcade in the hotel."},{"letter":"F","title":"The Padnal","text":"The Padnal is an older hotel in the heart of the city, with ground-floor rooms opening onto a courtyard-style garden. Prices are reasonable. There is a sports centre and a small cinema and nightclub. A band performs every evening in the hotel restaurant, where excellent French food is served. Airport buses pick up from the hotel."},{"letter":"G","title":"The Westmore Hotel","text":"The Westmore Hotel is in beautiful countryside to the east of the city. It is peaceful and inexpensive, although the accommodation is basic. There are opportunities nearby for sailing and diving, and a lot of interesting routes to explore on foot."},{"letter":"H","title":"The Grange Hotel","text":"Although the prices at the Grange Hotel are higher than at many city-centre hotels, it has a lot to offer. It shares a modern complex with nightclubs, cinemas, shops and conference facilities, 20 kilometres west of the centre. Trains run from the nearby railway station to the city centre and the airport."}]},{"part":3,"title":"ANTARCTICA SIGHTSEEING FLIGHTS WEBSITE","text":"During the brief Antarctic summer, Antarctica Sightseeing Flights takes tourists in a full-size passenger plane (Boeing 747) from Sydney, Australia, over the continent of Antarctica and back.\n\nFlight dates for next year are not definite yet, but if you make a booking now, your deposit is transferable if we need to change the day.\n\nHow long is the flight?\nThe average flight is 12 hours. About four hours into the flight, we usually see the first sea ice and icebergs. We spend four hours over Antarctica and the remaining time travelling home. On the way to and from the continent, Antarctica experts who have lived on scientific research stations there give talks on the environment and history, and answer questions. A camera man on the flight, which is linked to the video system on board, gives you a pilot's-eye view throughout, from take-off to landing.\n\nWill I get a seat next to a window?\nAll passengers are given two boarding passes. At the halfway point of the flight, passengers are asked to move to the seat shown on their second boarding pass. Business and first class passengers will have a window seat for half of the flight and a non-window seat for the other half. Economy passengers will have a window seat or the seat next to a window seat for half of the flight and spend the other half of the flight. While over Antarctica, we encourage passengers to get up from their seats and move about the plane, allowing everyone to share the sightseeing opportunity. The pilot flies in long 'figure 8s' over various points of interest to allow those amazing sights to be seen equally well from both sides of the plane.\n\nWhat happens if the weather is bad?\nWe have 17 different approved flight plans. Our captain looks at the satellite cloud picture on the morning of the flight and selects the direction which promises the clearest views.\n\nWill I see penguins or other wildlife?\nAs most Antarctic wildlife lives at sea level, over 3,000 metres below the plane, we can't see them in any detail. We do not land on Antarctica, primarily for environmental reasons. To experience Antarctic wildlife, you would need to join a boat tour.\n\nCan I take photographs?\nDefinitely! All cameras are welcome. We even provide a few fun onboard photo opportunities where you can experience what it is like to wear Antarctic cold weather clothes.","items":[{"n":11,"q":"Sightseeing flights to Antarctica are available for a limited period each year.","answer":"A"},{"n":12,"q":"You may have to book a flight without being certain when it will happen.","answer":"A"},{"n":13,"q":"Passengers have views of Antarctic scenery for more than half their flight.","answer":"B"},{"n":14,"q":"Passengers can speak to people who have experience of working in Antarctica.","answer":"A"},{"n":15,"q":"Special video equipment operates during the whole flight.","answer":"A"},{"n":16,"q":"People are asked to stay sitting down so that everybody can see better.","answer":"B"},{"n":17,"q":"There is an advantage in sitting on one particular side of the plane.","answer":"B"},{"n":18,"q":"Before departure, the pilot chooses between a number of possible routes.","answer":"A"},{"n":19,"q":"A special attraction of the trip is having clear views of Antarctic wildlife.","answer":"B"},{"n":20,"q":"Passengers have the chance to put on clothes designed for travel in Antarctica.","answer":"A"}]},{"part":4,"title":"Maria Mutola – Former 800 metres Olympic champion","text":"In 1988, Maria Mutola was playing football as the only girl in an all-boys team in a local competition in Mozambique. 'We won,' she said. 'At first no one thought it was a problem that I was a girl. But then the team we beat complained.'\n\nThe story appeared in a local newspaper and José Craveirinha, who had encouraged other African athletes, learnt about Maria. He went to meet her and found her kicking a ball around outside the football club. He realised immediately that she was fast. 'He talked to me about athletics. I had no idea what he meant. The only sport I knew about was football. Then he bought me running shoes and took me training. It was such hard work and my legs really ached.' But José visited her parents and persuaded them she could be successful and this would help end their poverty. They agreed to let him take her away to train.\n\nIn 1991, she finally accepted an invitation to train in the United States. She had refused previously because she knew she would miss her family. Her background was unlike those of the girls she met in the US. She explains, 'They were good athletes but, while I worried about my parents having enough to eat, they worried about dresses and make-up. They knew very little about me and even less about my problems. But I knew I was lucky to be there. The trainers were brilliant and I learnt a lot.'\n\nToday, Maria still runs and for most of the year she lives happily in South Africa with her mother.","items":[{"n":21,"q":"What is the writer trying to do in the text?","options":["persuade more Africans to take up athletics","describe how Maria became a top athlete","give information about Mozambique","explain how Maria manages to stay fit"],"answer":"B"},{"n":22,"q":"José Craveirinha found out about Maria when","options":["he went to watch a local football competition.","she was blamed for her team losing a football competition.","he saw an article about her role in a football match.","people complained about another member of her football team."],"answer":"C"},{"n":23,"q":"When José first introduced Maria to athletics, she","options":["didn't know what was involved.","was worried about being injured.","was keen to learn everything he knew.","didn't think her family would approve."],"answer":"A"},{"n":24,"q":"What does Maria say about the girls she met in the United States?","options":["They did not make full use of their abilities.","Their training programmes were less demanding than hers.","They did not show enough respect for the trainers.","Their experiences of life were very different from hers."],"answer":"D"},{"n":25,"q":"What would Maria say about her life?","options":["José has made all my dreams possible. From the first day we met, I was certain I wanted to become a top athlete.","My life hasn't always been easy but I've had many opportunities. Running is important to me and so is my family.","I regret becoming involved in athletics. It was horrible leaving Mozambique and my parents. I'd like to go back to football.","The US has some wonderful training facilities, so I'm glad that I agreed to go when I was first offered the chance."],"answer":"B"}]},{"part":5,"title":"HONEY","text":"Honey is a sweet liquid (0) ……… (made) by bees. It [26] of water and sugars. Bees may travel as [27] as seventy-five thousand kilometres and visit over two million flowers to produce just half a kilo of honey. The colour and flavour of honey depend [28] the type of flower visited. In [29] there are more than three hundred [30] of honey.\nThe lighter-coloured ones are generally milder in flavour than darker honey.\nIn ancient [31], honey was the main sweet food, as sugar was very [32]. Honey was of great [33] to the ancient Egyptians, who used it as payment.\nToday, honey is produced and eaten in [34] part of the world. Research suggests that it prevents tiredness and improves athletic performance. However, honey is not just food – it [35] be taken for sore throats and is used in many skin and hair-care products.","gaps":{"26":{"options":["involves","contains","includes","consists"],"answer":"D"},"27":{"options":["well","long","soon","far"],"answer":"D"},"28":{"options":["to","on","for","with"],"answer":"B"},"29":{"options":["case","order","fact","place"],"answer":"C"},"30":{"options":["varieties","collections","sets","differences"],"answer":"A"},"31":{"options":["seasons","times","years","dates"],"answer":"B"},"32":{"options":["distant","rare","small","slim"],"answer":"B"},"33":{"options":["cost","price","value","charge"],"answer":"C"},"34":{"options":["all","some","most","every"],"answer":"D"},"35":{"options":["shall","need","can","ought"],"answer":"C"}}}],"writing":[{"part":1,"title":"Here are some sentences about reading books.","items":[{"n":1,"first":"None of my friends enjoy reading as much as I do.","before":"I enjoy reading","after":"any of my friends.","answers":["much more than","more than"]},{"n":2,"first":"I borrowed a very good book from my teacher.","before":"My teacher","after":"me a very good book.","answers":["has lent","lent"]},{"n":3,"first":"It doesn't matter to me if a book is long or short.","before":"I don't","after":"if a book is long or short.","answers":["mind","care","worry"]},{"n":4,"first":"My mother finished War and Peace in only three weeks.","before":"It only","after":"my mother three weeks to finish War and Peace.","answers":["took"]},{"n":5,"first":"I am often given books as presents.","before":"People often","after":"me books as presents.","answers":["give"]}]},{"part":2,"items":[{"n":6,"kind":"message","task":"Richard, your English friend, has sent you some birthday money for you to buy a new DVD.\n\nWrite an email to Richard. In your email, you should\n• thank him for the present\n• tell him which DVD you are going to buy\n• explain why you have chosen this film.\n\nWrite 35–45 words on your answer sheet.","points":["ringraziare Richard per i soldi del compleanno","dire quale DVD vuoi comprare","spiegare perché hai scelto quel DVD"]}]},{"part":3,"items":[{"n":7,"kind":"letter","task":"This is part of a letter you receive from an English penfriend.\n\n'My sister's getting married next week and we are all excited about the wedding. Tell me about weddings in your country. What do people wear? Do they eat special food?'\n\nNow write a letter, answering your penfriend's questions.\nWrite your letter in about 100 words on your answer sheet."},{"n":8,"kind":"story","task":"Your teacher has asked you to write a story for homework.\nYour story must begin with this sentence:\n\nAs the concert finished, I heard someone call my name.\n\nWrite your story in about 100 words on your answer sheet."}]}],"listening":[{"part":1,"audio":"audio/pet5-t2-l1.mp3","items":[{"n":1,"q":"Which sport will the woman learn on holiday?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"C"},{"n":2,"q":"What does the girl's penfriend look like now?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"B"},{"n":3,"q":"Which animals did the children see?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"C"},{"n":4,"q":"Which TV programme is on first?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"B"},{"n":5,"q":"What does the boy decide to buy for his grandmother?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"A"},{"n":6,"q":"What time is the man's appointment?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"B"},{"n":7,"q":"What has the woman lost?","img":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["","",""],"answer":"B"}],"example":"img/pet5-t2-l1-ex.jpg"},{"part":2,"intro":"You will hear a man called Frank, talking on the radio about looking for ships that sank at sea long ago.","audio":"audio/pet5-t2-l2.mp3","items":[{"n":8,"q":"The first old ship which Frank found was","options":["covered by rocks.","older than he first thought.","easy to find."],"answer":"C"},{"n":9,"q":"Frank finds ships quickly because he","options":["reads history books.","is a professional diver.","uses the latest equipment."],"answer":"C"},{"n":10,"q":"What does Frank say about the ship called The Seabird?","options":["It was built in 1859.","It sank in a storm.","It was badly made."],"answer":"B"},{"n":11,"q":"Frank says his wedding ring","options":["was made from gold he found himself.","is worth £88,000.","was found by a friend."],"answer":"A"},{"n":12,"q":"Frank's wife believes he should","options":["stop diving.","give things to museums.","sell some of his collection."],"answer":"C"},{"n":13,"q":"How did Frank learn to dive?","options":["by joining a diving club","by going on a diving holiday","by teaching himself"],"answer":"A"}]},{"part":3,"intro":"You will hear a man telling a group of students about a trip to the theatre.","audio":"audio/pet5-t2-l3.mp3","notes":"Trip to Staunton Theatre\nMeet at 6.00 p.m. at the [14] of the school.\nThe name of the play is [15].\nGet a copy of the play from the [16].\nEach theatre ticket will cost £ [17].\nAfter the theatre – have [18] and coffee.\nOn the return journey, the coach will stop at the [19] and then the school.","answers":{"14":["back gate","the back gate"],"15":["The Party","Party"],"16":["school secretary","secretary","the school secretary","the secretary"],"17":["£15.75","15.75"],"18":["ice cream","ice-cream","icecream","ice creams"],"19":["bus station","bus stop","the bus station","the bus stop"]}},{"part":4,"intro":"You will hear a conversation between a teenage boy, Alex, and his sister, Rose, about where to go to eat.","audio":"audio/pet5-t2-l4.mp3","items":[{"n":20,"q":"Alex and Rose's mother have gone to their Grandad's.","answer":"B"},{"n":21,"q":"Alex tries to persuade Rose to cook supper.","answer":"B"},{"n":22,"q":"Alex wants to eat in an expensive restaurant.","answer":"A"},{"n":23,"q":"Alex likes some of the music at Classic Express.","answer":"A"},{"n":24,"q":"Alex has eaten at Classic Express before.","answer":"B"},{"n":25,"q":"Alex and Rose are going to walk to the restaurant together.","answer":"A"}]}]};
